<section class="lp-section" id="comparison"><div class="lp-wrap">
  <div class="lp-heading"><h2>Outside sales CRM comparison and pricing</h2><p>Prices are in USD before tax. The table flags separate CRM fees, add-ons and quotes.</p></div>
  {{ g.table('Ten outside sales tools compared for five reps',['Tool','Best fit','What reps get','Five-rep software cost'],guide.comparisonRows) }}
  <p class="lp-method"><strong>Prices and features checked {{ guide.reviewed }}.</strong></p>
</div></section>

<section class="lp-section lp-paper" id="costs"><div class="lp-wrap">
  <div class="lp-heading"><h2>Where does it make sense to pay more?</h2><p>Ask for the CRM, routing, integration and setup costs on one quote.</p></div>
  <div class="sg-flow">
    <div><h3>RepMove vs Map My Customers</h3><p>Map My Customers costs about $2,700 more per year for five reps on the plans shown. Test whether its offline features and territory reporting earn the difference.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Badger Maps vs Leadbeam</h3><p>Start with Badger if mileage is the issue. Leadbeam Pro costs $5,460 more per year for five reps. Demo it if poor visit notes also cost your team time.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Pipedrive vs Zoho CRM + RouteIQ</h3><p>The base CRMs cost the same on the plans shown. For scheduled days with several stops, compare the price of Zoho plus RouteIQ with Pipedrive plus a route planner.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dg-callout"><h3>What would the extra cost have to save?</h3><p>A $2,700 annual price difference works out to $54 a week over 50 selling weeks. At $45 an hour in staff time, the pricier tool needs to save the team 72 minutes a week.</p><p>Time the checklist below for both tools. Include any work needed to fix records after they sync.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section class="lp-section" id="reviews"><div class="lp-wrap">
  <div class="lp-heading"><h2>Which outside sales CRM should you buy?</h2></div>
  {{ g.providers(guide) }}
  <p class="dg-related">For a phone-first desk team, see our <a href="/resources/best-crm-for-outbound-sales/">outbound sales CRM comparison</a>. If you mainly need a first pipeline, compare <a href="/resources/best-crm-for-small-businesses/">CRMs for small businesses</a>.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="lp-section lp-dark" id="trial"><div class="lp-wrap">
  <div class="lp-heading"><h2>Try the CRM with 12 real accounts</h2><p>Give two reps the same stops and tasks in both tools.</p></div>
  <div class="sg-copy-layout">
    <pre id="sg-resource-text" tabindex="0" aria-label="12-account outside sales CRM trial checklist">1. Import 12 accounts. Include two contacts at one site and one bad address.
2. Build a route with fixed times. Cancel two stops and reorder the route.
3. Lose signal. Open a record, add a note and save a follow-up.
4. Reconnect. Check the account, owner and next task. Look for duplicates.
5. Hand an interested account to another rep. Ask them to explain the next step from the record alone.
6. Record the five-seat cost, setup fees and minutes spent fixing the output.</pre>
    <aside><h3>What earns a pass</h3><p>The next rep can find the conversation, the correct person and the agreed follow-up without texting the first rep.</p><button id="sg-copy-resource" class="lp-button" type="button" hidden>Copy the field trial checklist</button><span id="sg-copy-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span><noscript><p>Select and copy the checklist.</p></noscript></aside>
  </div>
</div></section>

